  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying which lottery is more right-skewed—defined as assigning greater probability to unfavorable outcomes—and preferring that one. As established, Lottery B is more right-skewed (25% loss vs. 20%), so the reason would favor B. However, the individual chose A and justified this by wanting to reduce the chance of a loss, which contradicts the reason's preference. Moreover, the individual does not reference any concept related to skewness, distribution shape, or relative concentration of probabilities across outcomes. Their focus is on minimizing loss probability, which is a component of skewness but interpreted inversely (they prefer less right-skew, not more). There is no evidence that the individual evaluated the lotteries in terms of skewness or followed the rule of preferring the more right-skewed option. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
