{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, where a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this context, 'unfavorable outcomes' are the negative monetary outcomes (-6000 and -3000), and the 'favorable' outcome is 0 (no loss). To assess skewness: Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of -6000 and 99.9% chance of 0. The distribution is highly right-skewed because the small probability mass is on a large loss (extreme left tail), and most of the probability is on 0. Similarly, Lottery B has a 0.2% chance of -3000 and 99.8% chance of 0. Its distribution also has a small left tail but with higher probability (0.2%) on a smaller loss. Comparing the two: Lottery B assigns twice the probability to a loss than A, but the loss is half as large. In terms of skewness, Lottery A has a more extreme (larger) loss with lower probability, which makes it more left-skewed (not right-skewed). Right-skew in losses would mean higher probability on worse outcomes. However, in loss-only lotteries (all outcomes ≤ 0), 'right-skew' would imply a long tail to the right (i.e., more mass on less negative values), which both lotteries exhibit. But the reason defines right-skew as 'greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes' — here, unfavorable is more negative. So, if more probability is on the worse loss, it's more right-skewed. But this contradicts standard skewness: higher probability on worse outcomes (larger losses) would actually make it left-skewed. There is ambiguity in the definition. However, interpreting the reason as intended: it prefers the lottery with more probability on worse outcomes. Lottery A has a worse outcome (-6000 vs -3000) but lower probability (0.1% vs 0.2%). So Lottery B has higher probability on a loss, but the loss is smaller. The net effect: Lottery B has more 'unfavorable' probability mass in the sense of higher chance of any loss, but A has a more severe unfavorable outcome. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, interpreted as the one with higher probability on worse outcomes. But since A has worse outcome but lower probability, and B has less bad outcome but higher probability, it's ambiguous which is more right-skewed under this non-standard definition. However, under the reason’s definition — 'greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes' — Lottery B assigns 0.2% to a loss vs 0.1% in A, so B has more probability on unfavorable outcomes. But A’s unfavorable outcome is worse. The reason does not weight severity, only probability portion. So if 'unfavorable outcome' is any loss, then B has higher probability (0.2% > 0.1%), so B is more right-skewed. If 'unfavorable' means the worst possible outcome, then A has the worst outcome, but with lower probability. The reason says 'a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes' — plural. So likely, it refers to the total probability of unfavorable (i.e., loss) outcomes. Both lotteries have only one unfavorable outcome. So Lottery B has higher probability (0.2%) on its unfavorable outcome than A (0.1%). Thus, B is more right-skewed under this definition. Therefore, the reason would prefer B. The reason is applicable: the necessary information (probabilities and outcomes) is available.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports: 'Although both cases are very unlikely, the -6000 in A deterred me. That's why I chose B, even though the probability of getting -3000 here is higher (0.2 vs 0.1) than in A.' Summary: The individual considered both the magnitude of the loss and its probability. They explicitly note the larger loss in A (-6000) as a deterrent, indicating that the severity of the worst outcome played a key role in their decision. They also acknowledge that B has a higher probability of loss (0.2% vs 0.1%), which they recognize as a downside, but they still chose B because the loss in A was too large. Their justification is based on aversion to the extreme loss in A, despite its lower probability. The reasoning is consistent with a focus on worst-case avoidance or loss aversion, particularly sensitivity to the maximum possible loss. They do not mention skewness, distribution shape, or probabilistic concentration on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the emphasis is on the salience of the -6000 outcome in A. The higher probability in B is noted but discounted due to the smaller loss amount.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, interpreted as the probability mass assigned to unfavorable outcomes. Under this, B has higher probability of loss (0.2% > 0.1%), so it is more right-skewed and preferred. The individual chose B, which matches the reason’s recommendation. However, the justification is not based on the higher probability of loss in B, but rather on the avoidance of the larger loss in A. The individual was deterred by the severity of the outcome in A, not attracted by the distributional shape of B. The information considered by the individual includes the magnitude of the worst loss and its probability, but the dominant factor was the magnitude. The decision reason, in contrast, emphasizes the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes, regardless of severity. The individual did not express preference for B because it has more probability on loss, but because A’s loss is too big. Thus, the rationale differs fundamentally. The choice aligns with the reason’s recommendation by coincidence, not due to the same reasoning process. There is no evidence the individual assessed skewness or compared the balance of probability across outcomes. Therefore, while the choice is consistent with the reason, the deliberation process described does not reflect the use of the skewness-based rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

