  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, i.e., one where a larger portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In Lottery A, there is a certain loss of 500 Euros (100% probability), so it has no variability and thus no skewness in the distribution of outcomes. Lottery B has a 50% chance of losing 1000 Euros (unfavorable) and 50% chance of losing nothing (favorable). Since both outcomes are non-positive, 'favorable' here would be the less negative outcome (0 Euros). In Lottery B, half the probability is on the relatively favorable outcome (0) and half on the unfavorable (-1000), whereas in Lottery A, the entire probability is on a moderate loss (-500). Thus, Lottery A assigns 100% probability to an outcome that is worse than the average of B's outcomes, but B spreads probability equally between a better and worse outcome. Right-skewness in losses typically implies a few high-loss outcomes and more frequent low-loss or neutral outcomes. Here, B has a 'better' outcome (0) with 50% probability and a 'worse' outcome (-1000) with 50%, so it is left-skewed in losses (risk of large loss), not right-skewed. Lottery A, being certain, has no skew. Therefore, neither lottery is clearly right-skewed, but if anything, B is left-skewed (tail to the left in the value distribution). The decision reason would prefer the more right-skewed lottery, which would be the one with more probability on worse outcomes. Lottery A is more concentrated on a moderately bad outcome, while B has a mix. However, since right-skew in losses means higher probability on bad outcomes, A could be seen as more right-skewed (100% on loss) vs B (50% on loss). But technically, skewness requires variation, so A has zero skew. Thus, the reason may not cleanly apply, but it could interpret A as more right-skewed due to certainty of loss. However, the reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would be A if we stretch the definition. But B is actually more variable with a chance of no loss, so it's less skewed in the right direction. In sum, the reason would likely prefer A (if considering concentration on loss as right-skew), but this is ambiguous. The relevant information for the reason includes the probability distribution of losses and the direction of skewness in outcomes.",
